<script type="text/javascript" src="4c732a32-b52f-11e9-9d71-0cc47a792c0a_id_4c732a32-b52f-11e9-9d71-0cc47a792c0a_files/wz_jsgraphics.js"></script>
<div style="position:absolute;left:50%;margin-left:-306px;top:0px;width:612px;height:792px;border-style:outset;overflow:hidden">
  <div style="position:absolute;left:0px;top:0px">
    <img src="/assets/images/reed-solomon/pdfImages/background01.jpg" width=612 height=792></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:87.00px;top:294.00px" class="cls_004"><span class="cls_004">On the Construction of Reed-Solomon Codes</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:264.75px;top:343.76px" class="cls_006"><span class="cls_006">Kaspar Poland</span></div>
</div>
<div style="position:absolute;left:50%;margin-left:-306px;top:802px;width:612px;height:792px;border-style:outset;overflow:hidden">
  <div style="position:absolute;left:0px;top:0px">
    <img src="/assets/images/reed-solomon/pdfImages/background02.jpg" width=612 height=792></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:246.00px;top:35.51px" class="cls_007"><span class="cls_007">Kaspar Poland</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:534.00px;top:35.51px" class="cls_007"><span class="cls_007">1</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:104.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">Reed-Solomon codes offer a solution to a very fundamental problem in information</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:120.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">theory and signal processing: </span><span class="cls_008">how can data be sent such that if the data gets corrupted, it can</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:137.25px" class="cls_008"><span class="cls_008">be fully recovered?</span><span class="cls_005"> Reed-Solomon codes, among other such codes, are one solution to the</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:153.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">problem of data corruption. The problem arises when there is noise within a </span><span class="cls_009">channel</span><span class="cls_005">. Data is</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:170.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">prepared on the sending end, and sent over said channel. The message is received on the</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:186.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">receiving end, and read. If the data is encoded on the sending end, when the receiver receives it,</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:203.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">the entire message is decoded to get the original data back. See </span><span class="cls_008">Figure 1.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:236.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">When presented with the problem, the first attempted solution might be to repeat</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:252.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">elements within the message. In a message ROME, repeat each letter for the result</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:269.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">RROOMMEE. If any one letter gets corrupted, the receiver can immediately know. Say, the</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:285.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">received message is HROOMMEE. The receiver is unsure if the message is ROME or HOME.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:302.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">The receiver concludes that an error in the first letter was made, but they are unsure of which</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:318.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">word was meant, and therefore cannot </span><span class="cls_009">correct</span><span class="cls_005"> the error, only </span><span class="cls_009">detect</span><span class="cls_005"> the error. The next idea</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:335.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">might be to repeat it three times, as the balanced effect might not happen. For the data ROME,</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:351.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">the sender would send RRROOOMMMEEE, which is called a </span><span class="cls_009">message</span><span class="cls_005">. Notice that any one</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:368.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">block can be corrupted with one error, and still be correctable. RRR might become RHR, but the</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:384.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">receiver could be more certain in the block being an R, rather than an H. Codes such as these are</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:401.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">called repetition codes, and are useful for understanding the problem that is attempting to be</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:417.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">solved. However, due to their abysmal code rates, they are not very useful in the field. </span><span class="cls_010">1 </span><span class="cls_005">of the</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:491.94px;top:425.85px" class="cls_010"><span class="cls_010">3</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:435.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">message are </span><span class="cls_009">information symbols</span><span class="cls_005">, and the other </span><span class="cls_010">2 </span><span class="cls_005">are considered </span><span class="cls_009">redundant symbols</span><span class="cls_005">, resulting</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:306.69px;top:443.10px" class="cls_010"><span class="cls_010">3</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:452.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">in a terrible code rate for the amount of data that can be recovered vs the amount of data that was</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:468.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">sent (Danziger, 2). The problem of coming up with efficient codes to transmit data now</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:485.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">becomes: </span><span class="cls_008">how does one append the least amount of </span><span class="cls_011">redundant symbols</span><span class="cls_008"> onto the end of the</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:501.75px" class="cls_011"><span class="cls_011">information symbols</span><span class="cls_008"> while retaining the ability to recover the data to a desired level.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:534.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">There are other problems with the repetition code used in the earlier example. Each letter</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:551.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">we used is really called a </span><span class="cls_009">codeword</span><span class="cls_005">. The goal is to make the codewords as different as possible,</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:567.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">in the case that we might be able to guess which one it is when an error occurs. This is obvious,</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:584.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">as a two character error between the words THIS and THAT would completely render any</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:600.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">decoder ineffective, while a two character error between THISABC and THATXYZ means the</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:617.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">words are still recoverable. In this simple example, THIS and THAT are the </span><span class="cls_009">information symbols</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:633.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">while ABC and XYZ are </span><span class="cls_009">redundant symbols</span><span class="cls_005"> used only to differentiate codewords form each</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:650.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">other. To achieve this goal, the codewords need structure. In the case of Reed-Solomon codes,</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:666.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">the structure comes from using mathematical structures. Finite fields, also called Galois fields,</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:683.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">represent codewords using polynomials and the properties that polynomials have.</span></div>
</div>
<div style="position:absolute;left:50%;margin-left:-306px;top:1604px;width:612px;height:792px;border-style:outset;overflow:hidden">
  <div style="position:absolute;left:0px;top:0px">
    <img src="/assets/images/reed-solomon/pdfImages/background03.jpg" width=612 height=792></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:246.00px;top:35.51px" class="cls_007"><span class="cls_007">Kaspar Poland</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:534.00px;top:35.51px" class="cls_007"><span class="cls_007">2</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:71.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">For ease of explanation, numbers are used to represent information. The numbers are in</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:87.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">binary, being either a 0 or a 1. A </span><span class="cls_009">bit</span><span class="cls_005"> of a binary number is the 1 or 0, while the binary number is</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:104.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">a string of consecutive of 1s and 0s. For example, 100110101 is a binary number, and the first </span><span class="cls_009">bit</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:120.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">of that binary number is a 1.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:153.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">Before beginning to get into the intricacies of how Reed-Solomon codes are constructed</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:170.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">and used, a few terms must be cleared up. Coding theory, pioneered by Richard Hamming and</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:186.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">Claude Shannon in the 1960’s, coined a lot of the terms that are still used today.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:219.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">Information is divided up by the encoder to process in chunks. Let </span><span class="cls_009">k</span><span class="cls_005"> be the amount of</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:236.10px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">information in each section. The </span><span class="cls_009">message</span><span class="cls_005"> is represented with length-</span><span class="cls_009"> k</span><span class="cls_005"> vectors, </span><span class="cls_009">b</span><span class="cls_005"> . There are 2</span><span class="cls_012"><sup>k</sup></span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:255.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">possible </span><span class="cls_009">messages.</span><span class="cls_005"> The </span><span class="cls_009">encoder</span><span class="cls_005"> takes each message and transforms it into a length-</span><span class="cls_009"> n</span><span class="cls_005"> vector </span><span class="cls_009">z</span><span class="cls_005">.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:272.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">Each vector </span><span class="cls_009">z</span><span class="cls_005"> is called a codeword. Each element within each vector </span><span class="cls_009">z</span><span class="cls_005"> is a symbol. Symbols</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:308.87px;top:288.60px" class="cls_012"><span class="cls_012">k</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:290.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">are both discrete and unique. Because there are 2</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:315.47px;top:290.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">possible messages, and each is transformed</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:220.92px;top:306.60px" class="cls_012"><span class="cls_012">k</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:308.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">independently, there are also 2</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:227.52px;top:308.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">possible codewords. The set of all possible codewords is called</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:325.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">an (</span><span class="cls_009">n</span><span class="cls_005">, </span><span class="cls_009">k</span><span class="cls_005">)</span><span class="cls_009"> block code</span><span class="cls_005">, or just the </span><span class="cls_009">code</span><span class="cls_005">.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:359.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">The code’s size becomes exponentially larger for larger and larger values of </span><span class="cls_009">k</span><span class="cls_005">. This was</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:376.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">a large problem in terms of the applicability of Reed-Solomon codes, as they were often too</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:393.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">large to be able to compute efficiently while still retaining their error-correction properties.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:409.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">Decoding at the end of transmission involves looking through every possibility and choosing the</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:426.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">codeword that is the most likely to be correct. This can be very computationally expensive (time</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:442.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">consuming or computationally inefficient) with large values of </span><span class="cls_009">k</span><span class="cls_005">, and therefore more efficient</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:459.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">algorithms are used today.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:492.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">The definition of the </span><span class="cls_009">Hamming distance, d</span><span class="cls_005">(</span><span class="cls_009">x</span><span class="cls_005">, </span><span class="cls_009">y</span><span class="cls_005">), between two codewords is the amount</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:510.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">of data bits that need to be changed in order for one codeword to become the other. For example,</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:276.29px;top:525.60px" class="cls_012"><span class="cls_012">nd</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:73.98px;top:527.25px" class="cls_009"><span class="cls_009">d</span><span class="cls_005">(110, 010) = 1 , because the bits in the 2</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:287.29px;top:527.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">and</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:309.60px;top:525.60px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">3</span><span class="cls_012"><sup>rd</sup></span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:328.90px;top:527.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">positions need to flip in order for the two</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:544.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">vectors to be equal to each other. The order of the function does not matter, as for any </span><span class="cls_009">x</span><span class="cls_005"> and </span><span class="cls_009">y</span><span class="cls_005">,</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:301.46px;top:559.35px" class="cls_012"><span class="cls_012">n</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:311.04px;top:564.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">|</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:76.98px;top:567.75px" class="cls_009"><span class="cls_009">d</span><span class="cls_005">(</span><span class="cls_009">x</span><span class="cls_005">, </span><span class="cls_009">y</span><span class="cls_005">) =</span><span class="cls_009"> d</span><span class="cls_005">(</span><span class="cls_009">y</span><span class="cls_005">, </span><span class="cls_009">x</span><span class="cls_005">). Formally, this is written as</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:299.27px;top:567.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">∑</span></div>
