  <div style="position:absolute;left:159.00px;top:611.25px" class="cls_008"><span class="cls_008">Figure 3</span><span class="cls_005">: Possible codewords in the repetition code example</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:627.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">This is our code. Notice, again, our codewords are unique. In this case,</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:73.98px;top:644.25px" class="cls_009"><span class="cls_009">d </span><span class="cls_005">= 3, </span><span class="cls_009">t </span><span class="cls_005">=</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:159.17px;top:644.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">. This aligns with what we found earlier, with our eyes, as well as with the</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:125.22px;top:643.35px" class="cls_015"><span class="cls_015">d</span><span class="cls_016">−1</span><span class="cls_005"> = 1</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:130.03px;top:652.35px" class="cls_010"><span class="cls_010">2</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:662.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">letter example. It means that our code can correct one error.</span></div>
</div>
<div style="position:absolute;left:50%;margin-left:-306px;top:4010px;width:612px;height:792px;border-style:outset;overflow:hidden">
  <div style="position:absolute;left:0px;top:0px">
    <img src="/assets/images/reed-solomon/pdfImages/background06.jpg" width=612 height=792></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:246.00px;top:35.51px" class="cls_007"><span class="cls_007">Kaspar Poland</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:534.00px;top:35.51px" class="cls_007"><span class="cls_007">5</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:71.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">To discuss the construction of Reed-Solomon codes, the structure needs to be addressed</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:87.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">again. Up until this point, the symbols of the codewords that we have used are the binary</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:104.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">symbols, that is, 0 and 1. These symbols do not necessarily have to be binary. They can be any</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:120.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">symbols taken out of a finite field. Finite fields were pioneered by Évariste Galois, and are</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:137.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">fundamental to coding theory and its applications (Cox, 1). Because of their founder, they are</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:153.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">also called Galois Fields, and are denoted by </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (</span><span class="cls_009">q</span><span class="cls_005">), where </span><span class="cls_009">q</span><span class="cls_005"> is the order, or size, of the field.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:171.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">All finite fields either have a </span><span class="cls_009">prime subfield</span><span class="cls_005">, or are a prime subfield. Prime subfields are finite</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:187.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">fields with a </span><span class="cls_009">prime order</span><span class="cls_005">. This means that they are Galois Field </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (</span><span class="cls_009">p</span><span class="cls_005">), where </span><span class="cls_009">p</span><span class="cls_005"> is prime. Any</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:204.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">field </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (</span><span class="cls_009">p</span><span class="cls_012">n</span><span class="cls_005">) where</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:174.73px;top:204.75px" class="cls_009"><span class="cls_009">n</span><span class="cls_005">>1</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:205.13px;top:204.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">has the prime subfield</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:316.41px;top:204.75px" class="cls_009"><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (</span><span class="cls_009">p</span><span class="cls_005">).</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:356.07px;top:204.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">This can be seen by if</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:466.01px;top:204.75px" class="cls_009"><span class="cls_009">n </span><span class="cls_005">> 1,</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:499.41px;top:204.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">the</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:222.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">prime subfield IS the field, which means the field does not have its own, separate, prime</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:238.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">subfield. Prime subfields are important to the understanding of finite fields, as they are used to</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:255.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">represent the coefficients of various polynomials within the finite field.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:288.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">There are three classes of polynomials that are important to the understanding,</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:304.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">construction, and application of finite fields. They are called generator polynomials, primitive</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:321.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">polynomials, and minimal polynomials.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:354.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">The first class of polynomials are called called generator polynomials. Generator</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:370.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">polynomials are special in that over any prime finite field </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (</span><span class="cls_009">p</span><span class="cls_005">) , each element α of the finite</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:387.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">field is a root of the generator polynomial. It is called a generator simply due to the fact that</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:404.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">using the properties, it is possible to construct the entire finite field knowing the generator</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:420.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">polynomial and the size of the desired finite field. The generator also has cyclic properties,</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:437.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">which is important for Reed-Solomon codes, as they are a type of code called a </span><span class="cls_009">cyclic</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:453.75px" class="cls_009"><span class="cls_009">error-correcting code</span><span class="cls_005">. The generator polynomial is also important, as every codeword is </span><span class="cls_009">exactly</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:470.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">divisible by the generator polynomial, when the codewords are represented in polynomial form.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:503.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">The construction of the generator polynomial is put simply. The roots of the generator</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:519.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">polynomial must be the elements of the finite field that the code is working in, so the</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:536.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">construction using the primitive element is simple.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:277.36px;top:568.35px" class="cls_012"><span class="cls_012">q</span><span class="cls_010">−</span><span class="cls_012">k</span><span class="cls_010">+1</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:283.59px;top:577.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">∏ (</span><span class="cls_009">x</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:311.48px;top:576.60px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">−α</span><span class="cls_012"><sup>i</sup></span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:330.16px;top:577.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">)</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:282.63px;top:591.60px" class="cls_012"><span class="cls_012">i</span><span class="cls_010">=0</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:619.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">The second type of polynomial that will become useful in the construction of</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:636.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">Reed-Solomon codes are called primitive polynomials. They are polynomials in a subclass called</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:652.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">irreducible polynomials. All primitive polynomials are irreducible polynomials, but not every</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:669.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">irreducible polynomial can be primitive. In a finite field </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (</span><span class="cls_009">q</span><span class="cls_005">), where </span><span class="cls_009">q</span><span class="cls_005"> =</span><span class="cls_009"> p</span><span class="cls_012">k</span><span class="cls_005">,</span><span class="cls_009"> p</span><span class="cls_005"> is prime, and</span><span class="cls_009"> k</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:686.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">is any positive integer, the primitive polynomial will have coefficients that are elements in</span></div>
</div>
<div style="position:absolute;left:50%;margin-left:-306px;top:4812px;width:612px;height:792px;border-style:outset;overflow:hidden">
  <div style="position:absolute;left:0px;top:0px">
    <img src="/assets/images/reed-solomon/pdfImages/background07.jpg" width=612 height=792></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:246.00px;top:35.51px" class="cls_007"><span class="cls_007">Kaspar Poland</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:534.00px;top:35.51px" class="cls_007"><span class="cls_007">6</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:73.98px;top:71.25px" class="cls_009"><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (</span><span class="cls_009">p</span><span class="cls_005">), which is the prime subfield of </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (</span><span class="cls_009">q</span><span class="cls_005">). Primitive polynomials are defined by the fact</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:88.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">and property that they are polynomials with coefficients in the </span><span class="cls_009">prime subfield </span><span class="cls_005">of any field</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:73.98px;top:105.00px" class="cls_009"><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (</span><span class="cls_009">q</span><span class="cls_005">) in which the primitive element of </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (</span><span class="cls_009">q</span><span class="cls_005">) is not a root. For fields that have multiple</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:122.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">primitive elements, the primitive polynomial does not have a root of any of them. As an</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:139.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">example, the primitive polynomials of the finite field </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (8), or </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (2</span><span class="cls_010">3</span><span class="cls_005">), will have coefficients</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:156.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">that are in </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (2) , that is, either a 1 or a 0. </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (2) is also called the binary finite field, and</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:174.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">polynomials over the binary finite field are called binary polynomials. This makes the primitive</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:190.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">polynomials of any field with prime </span><span class="cls_009">p</span><span class="cls_005"> = 2 binary polynomials. The primitive polynomial for</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:73.98px;top:207.75px" class="cls_009"><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (8) is </span><span class="cls_009">p</span><span class="cls_005">(</span><span class="cls_009">x</span><span class="cls_005">) =</span><span class="cls_009"> x</span><span class="cls_010">3</span><span class="cls_005"> +</span><span class="cls_009">x</span><span class="cls_005">+1</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:201.96px;top:207.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">. These fields are especially useful as they closely resemble the</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:225.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">architecture used within computers, and therefore the operations are very fast to use. The simple</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:241.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">addition and multiplication tables for </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (2) are shown below.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:171.00px;top:402.75px" class="cls_008"><span class="cls_008">Figure 4</span><span class="cls_005">: Addition and multiplication tables for </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (2)</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:436.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">The third class of polynomials that will be useful to Reed-Solomon codes are called</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:453.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">minimal polynomials. They are polynomials that are used to represent the members of any finite</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:469.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">field. There is a unique polynomial for each element within a finite field, making this</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:486.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">representation useful for arithmetic operations on elements within the field. The arithmetic</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:502.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">operations are defined using one of the field’s primitive polynomials, of which there may be</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:519.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">many, but there is always guaranteed to be one. All arithmetic is defined using each elements'</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:535.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">polynomial representation, and it is all performed modulo a primitive polynomial of the field. In</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:552.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">arithmetic, the polynomial result is always reduced using the modulo of the primitive</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:568.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">polynomial, and then the coefficients are reduced using an integer, the </span><span class="cls_009">prime order</span><span class="cls_005"> of the field.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:585.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">This is why when adding 1 + 1 in </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (4) is 0, even though 2 is an element within </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (4). For</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:602.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">example, in </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (8), let us add 6 and 7. The polynomial representations of those two elements</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:619.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">are α</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:123.19px;top:619.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">and</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:201.13px;top:619.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">respectively. Adding them, the result is</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:97.95px;top:619.35px" class="cls_010"><span class="cls_010"><sup>2</sup></span><span class="cls_005"> +α</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:462.05px;top:619.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">The result of</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:145.49px;top:619.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">α</span><span class="cls_010">2</span><span class="cls_005"> +α+1</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:193.10px;top:619.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">,</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:394.41px;top:619.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">2α</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:429.61px;top:619.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">α</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:406.73px;top:619.35px" class="cls_010"><span class="cls_010"><sup>2</sup></span><span class="cls_005"> +2</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:438.59px;top:619.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">+1</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:636.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">any addition will always have a degree less than that of the primitive polynomial, therefore,</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:653.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">taking the result modulo the primitive polynomial is not necessary. This is analogous to asking</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:669.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">for the remainder of dividing 8 by 13. It will always be 8 if the denominator is greater.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:686.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">Therefore, the result is taken modulo the </span><span class="cls_009">prime order</span><span class="cls_005">, which, for </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (8), is 2. Therefore,</span></div>
</div>
<div style="position:absolute;left:50%;margin-left:-306px;top:5614px;width:612px;height:792px;border-style:outset;overflow:hidden">
  <div style="position:absolute;left:0px;top:0px">
    <img src="/assets/images/reed-solomon/pdfImages/background08.jpg" width=612 height=792></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:246.00px;top:35.51px" class="cls_007"><span class="cls_007">Kaspar Poland</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:534.00px;top:35.51px" class="cls_007"><span class="cls_007">7</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:73.98px;top:71.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">2</span><span class="cls_009">x</span><span class="cls_010">2</span><span class="cls_005"> +2</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.19px;top:71.25px" class="cls_009"><span class="cls_009">x</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:116.17px;top:71.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">+1</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:137.60px;top:71.25px" class="cls_009"><span class="cls_009">mod</span><span class="cls_005"> (2)</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:181.58px;top:71.25px" class="cls_014"><span class="cls_014">⇒</span><span class="cls_005">1. Notice that the result of 1 is not the number 1, but the polynomial</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:88.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">representation, which just so happens to equal the number 1. The addition of 4 and 7, for</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:105.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">instance, returns α + 1, which in </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (8), equals the number 2.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:138.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">This means the field is binary, and that the coefficients of the primitive polynomial will</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:155.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">be similarly binary, that is, either a 0 or a 1. The field has a primitive polynomial</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:73.98px;top:171.75px" class="cls_009"><span class="cls_009">p</span><span class="cls_005">(</span><span class="cls_009">x</span><span class="cls_005">)</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:99.62px;top:171.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">= </span><span class="cls_009">x</span><span class="cls_010">3</span><span class="cls_005"> +</span><span class="cls_009">x</span><span class="cls_005">+1</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:158.32px;top:171.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">. Addition and subtraction are the same operation within this field, due to the</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:189.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">properties of binary arithmetic. By definition, the primitive polynomial must have the primitive</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:205.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">element of the finite field as a root, so </span><span class="cls_009">p</span><span class="cls_005">(α)</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:285.88px;top:205.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">= 0. Using this, the primitive polynomial can be</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:222.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">rewritten in the form of what I will call a primitive identity of the finite field. Each primitive</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:239.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">polynomial has its own primitive identity, of which there can be multiple per finite field. The</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:255.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">primitive identity of </span><span class="cls_009">p</span><span class="cls_005">(</span><span class="cls_009">x</span><span class="cls_005">) is 0 = α</span><span class="cls_010">3</span><span class="cls_005"> +α+1</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:276.98px;top:255.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">, or</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:297.95px;top:255.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">α</span><span class="cls_010">3</span><span class="cls_005"> =α</span><span class="cls_010">2</span><span class="cls_005"> +1</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:289.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">Recalling that the primitive element is called the generator element of the finite field, and</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:306.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">raising it to consecutive powers generates every element within the finite field, the following</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:322.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">table can be constructed using </span><span class="cls_009">p</span><span class="cls_005">(</span><span class="cls_009">x</span><span class="cls_005">) =</span><span class="cls_009"> x</span><span class="cls_010">3</span><span class="cls_005"> +</span><span class="cls_009">x</span><span class="cls_005">+1as a primitive polynomial over</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:454.22px;top:322.50px" class="cls_009"><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (8)</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:89.25px;top:362.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">Exponential Representation</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:241.50px;top:362.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">Polynomial Representation</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:393.75px;top:362.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">Numerical Representation</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:89.25px;top:390.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">0</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:241.50px;top:390.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">0</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:393.75px;top:390.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">0</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:91.23px;top:417.60px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">α</span><span class="cls_010"><sup>0</sup></span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:241.50px;top:417.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">1</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:393.75px;top:417.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">1</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:91.23px;top:446.10px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">α</span><span class="cls_010"><sup>1</sup></span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:243.48px;top:446.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">α</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:393.75px;top:446.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">2</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:91.23px;top:474.60px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">α</span><span class="cls_010"><sup>2</sup></span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:243.48px;top:474.60px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">α</span><span class="cls_010"><sup>2</sup></span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:393.75px;top:474.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">4</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:91.23px;top:503.10px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">α</span><span class="cls_010"><sup>3</sup></span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:243.48px;top:503.10px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">α</span><span class="cls_010"><sup>2</sup></span><span class="cls_005"> + 1</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:393.75px;top:503.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">5</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:91.23px;top:531.60px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">α</span><span class="cls_010"><sup>4</sup></span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:243.48px;top:531.60px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">α</span><span class="cls_010"><sup>2</sup></span><span class="cls_005"> + α + 1</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:393.75px;top:531.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">7</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:91.23px;top:560.10px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">α</span><span class="cls_010"><sup>5</sup></span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:243.48px;top:560.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">α+1</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:393.75px;top:560.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">3</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:91.23px;top:588.60px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">α</span><span class="cls_010"><sup>6</sup></span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:243.48px;top:588.60px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">α</span><span class="cls_010"><sup>2</sup></span><span class="cls_005"> + α</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:393.75px;top:588.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">6</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:92.25px;top:612.00px" class="cls_008"><span class="cls_008">Table 1</span><span class="cls_005">: Expansion of elements into polynomial and numerical representation in </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (8)</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:645.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">Each exponential representation is taken modulo the primitive polynomial. For a complex</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:662.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">example, take α</span><span class="cls_010">6</span><span class="cls_005">.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:161.73px;top:662.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">α</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:168.05px;top:662.10px" class="cls_010"><span class="cls_010"><sup>6</sup></span><span class="cls_005"> =α</span><span class="cls_010"><sup>3</sup></span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:237.19px;top:662.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">α</span><span class="cls_010">2</span><span class="cls_005"> +1)(α</span><span class="cls_010">2</span><span class="cls_005"> +1)= α</span><span class="cls_010">4</span><span class="cls_005"> +2α</span><span class="cls_010">2</span><span class="cls_005"> +1=α</span><span class="cls_010">2</span><span class="cls_005"> +α+2=α</span><span class="cls_010">2</span><span class="cls_005"> +α</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:487.64px;top:662.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">This same</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:200.02px;top:662.10px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">*α</span><span class="cls_010"><sup>3</sup></span><span class="cls_005"> =(</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:679.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">procedure of reducing modulo the primitive identity can be seen for every exponential</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:696.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">representation of our primitive element.</span></div>
</div>
<div style="position:absolute;left:50%;margin-left:-306px;top:6416px;width:612px;height:792px;border-style:outset;overflow:hidden">
  <div style="position:absolute;left:0px;top:0px">
    <img src="/assets/images/reed-solomon/pdfImages/background09.jpg" width=612 height=792></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:246.00px;top:35.51px" class="cls_007"><span class="cls_007">Kaspar Poland</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:534.00px;top:35.51px" class="cls_007"><span class="cls_007">8</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:87.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">The numerical representation in </span><span class="cls_008">Table 1</span><span class="cls_005"> can be evaluated by using a known numerical</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:104.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">primitive element, rather than the character α. For the finite field </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (8), there is only one</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:121.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">primitive element, which is the number 2. Other finite fields may have many, but it is guaranteed</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:138.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">to have at least one.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:171.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">In the original approach of Reed-Solomon codes, the construction was fairly simple.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:187.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">Consider a </span><span class="cls_009">k</span><span class="cls_005"> -length set of information symbols. They are called symbols because they are taken</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:204.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">from a finite field </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (</span><span class="cls_009">q</span><span class="cls_005">). Let us notate them with {</span><span class="cls_009">m</span><span class="cls_005"> ,</span><span class="cls_009"> m</span><span class="cls_005"> ,</span><span class="cls_009"> m</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:384.50px;top:204.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">, ...,</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:409.46px;top:204.75px" class="cls_009"><span class="cls_009">m</span><span class="cls_012">k</span><span class="cls_010">−1</span><span class="cls_005">}. Then, consider a</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:334.73px;top:212.10px" class="cls_010"><span class="cls_010">0</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:356.17px;top:212.10px" class="cls_010"><span class="cls_010">1</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:377.61px;top:212.10px" class="cls_010"><span class="cls_010">2</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:222.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">polynomial </span><span class="cls_009">P</span><span class="cls_005"> (</span><span class="cls_009">x</span><span class="cls_005">) such that </span><span class="cls_009">P</span><span class="cls_005"> (</span><span class="cls_009">x</span><span class="cls_005">) =</span><span class="cls_009"> m</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:281.65px;top:222.00px" class="cls_009"><span class="cls_009">x</span><span class="cls_005">+</span><span class="cls_009">m x</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:322.95px;top:221.85px" class="cls_010"><span class="cls_010"><sup>2</sup></span><span class="cls_005"> + ... + </span><span class="cls_009">m</span><span class="cls_012">k</span><span class="cls_010">−1</span><span class="cls_012">k</span><span class="cls_010">−1</span><span class="cls_005">. Each codeword </span><span class="cls_009">c </span><span class="cls_005">in the</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:251.31px;top:222.00px" class="cls_010"><span class="cls_010">0</span><span class="cls_005"> +</span><span class="cls_009"><sup>m</sup></span><span class="cls_010">1</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:310.73px;top:229.35px" class="cls_010"><span class="cls_010">2</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:239.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">code </span><span class="cls_009">C</span><span class="cls_005"> is created by evaluating the polynomial using each element of the field </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (</span><span class="cls_009">q</span><span class="cls_005">). Because</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:490.03px;top:256.35px" class="cls_012"><span class="cls_012">q</span><span class="cls_010">−1</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:256.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">each codeword </span><span class="cls_009">c</span><span class="cls_005"> is a vector, the codeword is defined as {</span><span class="cls_009">P</span><span class="cls_005"> (0), </span><span class="cls_009">P</span><span class="cls_005"> (α</span><span class="cls_010">0</span><span class="cls_005">),</span><span class="cls_009"> P</span><span class="cls_005"> (α</span><span class="cls_010">1</span><span class="cls_005">), ...,</span><span class="cls_009"> P</span><span class="cls_005"> (α</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:504.04px;top:256.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">)}.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:273.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">Then, all codewords in </span><span class="cls_009">C</span><span class="cls_005"> are created by letting </span><span class="cls_009">k</span><span class="cls_005"> cycle through every possible value in </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (</span><span class="cls_009">q</span><span class="cls_005">).</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:309.90px;top:289.35px" class="cls_012"><span class="cls_012">m</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:291.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">In a binary field, that is, a field of the form </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (2</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:316.56px;top:291.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">), there are only two values, so logically it</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:309.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">follows that the code would have 2</span><span class="cls_012">k </span><span class="cls_005">codewords. A code generated from a</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:433.10px;top:309.00px" class="cls_009"><span class="cls_009">k</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:440.81px;top:309.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">-length information</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:326.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">packet using symbols from a finite field </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (</span><span class="cls_009">q</span><span class="cls_005">) will have </span><span class="cls_009">q</span><span class="cls_012">k</span><span class="cls_005"> different codewords.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:360.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">Another way of constructing the codewords of a code </span><span class="cls_009">C</span><span class="cls_005"> is using the generator</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:377.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">polynomial. The generator is by definition a perfect divisor of every codeword within C, and</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:393.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">when any semblance of the field </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (</span><span class="cls_009">q</span><span class="cls_005">) is gone, the codeword is simply the polynomial</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:411.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">multiplication of the generator polynomial </span><span class="cls_009">g</span><span class="cls_005">(</span><span class="cls_009">x</span><span class="cls_005">) and the information block </span><span class="cls_009">i</span><span class="cls_005">(</span><span class="cls_009">x</span><span class="cls_005">). The information</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:428.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">block is simply a </span><span class="cls_009">k</span><span class="cls_005"> -length string of information symbols. As an example, using the symbols</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:445.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">from the prime binary field </span><span class="cls_009">GF</span><span class="cls_005"> (2), the information block is simply a set length binary vector.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:462.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">This length </span><span class="cls_009">k</span><span class="cls_005"> defined a Reed-Solomon code (</span><span class="cls_009">n</span><span class="cls_005">, </span><span class="cls_009">k</span><span class="cls_005">).</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:496.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">A Reed-Solomon code is a </span><span class="cls_009">cyclic code</span><span class="cls_005">, which means that shifting the coefficients of any</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:513.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">codeword results in another codeword. This follows logically from the fact that all terms have</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:529.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">every possible value of the information packet, just in different codewords. For example,</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:546.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">consider a codeword in a cyclic code represented by the binary vector 1101. Shifting each bit to</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:562.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">the right and looping them, we arrive at 1110, which, by definition, must also be a codeword in</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:579.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">the cyclic code.</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:612.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">As Reed-Solomon codes are a type of error correction called </span><span class="cls_009">forward error correction</span><span class="cls_005">,</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:628.50px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">that is, the data is prepared before it is sent, the data must be encoded. In </span><span class="cls_008">Figure 1</span><span class="cls_005">, this can be</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:645.00px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">seen as transforming the data X into codeword β. This process needs to transform the </span><span class="cls_009">k</span><span class="cls_005"> -length</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:662.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">information block into codewords that the decoder can recognize.</span></div>
</div>
<div style="position:absolute;left:50%;margin-left:-306px;top:7218px;width:612px;height:792px;border-style:outset;overflow:hidden">
  <div style="position:absolute;left:0px;top:0px">
    <img src="/assets/images/reed-solomon/pdfImages/background10.jpg" width=612 height=792></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:246.00px;top:35.51px" class="cls_007"><span class="cls_007">Kaspar Poland</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:534.00px;top:35.51px" class="cls_007"><span class="cls_007">9</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:108.00px;top:71.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">Reed-Solomon codes did not become widespread and usable until significant advances in</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:87.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">their decoding was discovered. Originally, the decoders used exhaustive searches, looking for the</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:104.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">closest codeword to the codeword that was received in error. This is very inefficient, as with</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:120.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">bigger and bigger codes, it would take exponentially longer to complete. The key advance in the</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:137.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">decoding was made with the Berlekamp-Massey Algorithm, developed in 1967 by Elwyn</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:153.75px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">Berlekamp originally, and optimized by James Massey. It was a linear equation solver, and it</span></div>
  <div style="position:absolute;left:72.00px;top:170.25px" class="cls_005"><span class="cls_005">improved on the exponential nature of the exhaustive searches used originally.</span></div>
</div>
